--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (3/4 key facts kept) ---
I pawned my husband's watch to raise ten shillings towards paying the rent. Bert gave me his half of the rent and his board. The New Year begins gloomily, for more than two and a half million workers are out of employment, and the coal and cotton trades are in dispute. I hope that the dispute in South Wales will be settled to-morrow, so that the 150,000 miners who have struck work may return to it.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (5/5 key facts kept) ---
To-day I spoke with young Mr. Fenn about the day after New Year's Day, and resolved never to enter a wrong figure in the ledger. I entered the correct total in the ledger, 5115. I entered a wrong figure, 5051, in the ledger. I resolved never to enter a wrong figure in the ledger, and was paid my week's wages, £3 10s.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (0/4 key facts kept) ---
1. Read optimistically in The Times of a temporary settlement of the South Wales mining dispute.
   ☾ Ada (forgotten by morning): She dreamt that she was scrubbing an office, and found a purse full of gold.
   ☾ Arthur: He dreamt that he was standing in a great plain, and that all the inhabitants of the earth were gathered together before him; and he saw that each man held up his hand, and lo!  all the hands were black.
   ☾ Mr. Thomas: He dreamt that he promoted Hale to be chief clerk in the place of himself, Walter Thomas, and that Hale kissed the hand which raised him.

